
Step 1 — Identify the publication
Find and verify:
* Exact paper title
* Authors
* Publication year
* DOI
* Publisher
* Exact journal name
* Volume, issue, and pages/article number
* Publication type (journal article, conference paper, review, etc.)
Step 2 — Verify the journal
Determine:
* Whether it is a journal or conference
* ISSN/eISSN if available
* Publisher
* Scopus indexing status
* Web of Science indexing status, if applicable
* Journal quartile (Q1/Q2/Q3/Q4)
* Which subject category the quartile belongs to
* Whether the Q1/Q2/Q3/Q4 status applies to the relevant publication year
* CiteScore, Impact Factor/JCR information, if available
* Whether the journal is currently active
* Any relevant journal-quality concerns
Do not guess the quartile. If different databases or subject categories give different rankings, show them separately and explain the difference.
Step 3 — Verify the paper itself
Analyze:
* Research problem
* Research objective
* Research question, if stated
* Dataset(s)
* Method/model/algorithm
* Baseline methods
* Evaluation metrics
* Main results
* Main contribution
* Limitations
* Research gap addressed
* Whether the methodology is reproducible
* Whether the paper is relevant to my MSc thesis topic
Step 4 — Check research relevance
My research area is:
IoT intrusion detection using ML

Step 5 — Source verification
Use reliable sources and prioritize:
* Official journal/publisher page
* Scopus
* Web of Science
* Journal Citation Reports (if available)
* Crossref
* Official DOI record
For every important factual claim, provide the source.
